<body>
  <Header />
  <main>
  <article class="container">
    <header class="article-header">
      <span class="tag">Website Builders</span>
      <h1>Squarespace vs Wix: Which Website Builder Is Better in 2026?</h1>
      <p class="meta">Updated July 2026</p>
    </header>

    <p class="lead">
      Squarespace and Wix are the two website builders most often shortlisted in 2026, and they take opposite approaches. Squarespace wins on polished templates, a clean editor, and predictable design output. Wix wins on drag-and-drop flexibility, a larger app market, and lower entry pricing. This comparison breaks down templates, editor experience, pricing, e-commerce, SEO, and support so you can pick the right one for your next project.
    </p>

    <div class="cta-top">
      <a href="https://www.squarespace.com" class="btn btn-primary" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Try Squarespace Free</a>
      <a href="https://www.wix.com" class="btn btn-secondary" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Try Wix Free</a>
    </div>

    <h2>Quick Comparison</h2>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <tr><th>Feature</th><th>Squarespace</th><th>Wix</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Best For</td><td>Portfolios, brand-led sites</td><td>Small businesses, custom layouts</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Starting Price</td><td>$16/mo (Personal)</td><td>$17/mo (Light)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Free Tier</td><td>14-day trial only</td><td>Yes, with Wix branding</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Template Quality</td><td>Best in class, designer-made</td><td>800+ templates, variable quality</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Editor Type</td><td>Structured grid editor</td><td>Drag-and-drop, free-form</td></tr>
        <tr><td>App Market</td><td>Limited, curated</td><td>500+ apps, broad</td></tr>
        <tr><td>E-commerce</td><td>Yes, integrated</td><td>Yes, with more apps</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Transaction Fees</td><td>0% on commerce plans</td><td>0% on business plans</td></tr>
        <tr><td>SEO Tools</td><td>Good, clean URLs</td><td>Better, more granular</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mobile Editor</td><td>Responsive auto-layout</td><td>Separate mobile editor</td></tr>
        <tr><td>AI Site Builder</td><td>Squarespace Blueprint AI</td><td>Wix ADI and AI</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Email Marketing</td><td>Built-in (Email Campaigns)</td><td>Wix Email Marketing</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Customer Support</td><td>24/7 chat and email</td><td>24/7 chat and phone</td></tr>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>What Is Squarespace?</h2>
    <p>Squarespace is a website builder founded in 2003 by Anthony Casalena in his dorm room at the University of Maryland. It is known for designer-made templates, a structured grid editor that produces clean layouts automatically, and a focus on visual branding. Squarespace targets creative professionals, restaurants, and small businesses that want a polished site without hiring a designer.</p>
    <p>The current version, Squarespace 7.1, uses a unified template system that lets you switch templates without losing content. It includes built-in tools for blogging, e-commerce, email marketing, scheduling, and memberships. Squarespace Blueprint AI generates a custom site based on a few questions about your business. The platform is fully hosted, includes SSL, and offers 24/7 chat and email support. Pricing starts at $16 per month for Personal and goes up to $65 per month for Advanced Commerce.</p>

    <h2>What Is Wix?</h2>
    <p>Wix is a website builder founded in 2006 by Avishai Abrahami, Nadav Abrahami, and Giora Kaplan. It pioneered the drag-and-drop editor that lets users place elements anywhere on a page, free from a structured grid. Wix targets small businesses, freelancers, and anyone who wants maximum control over layout without writing code.</p>
    <p>The current version offers two editors: Wix Editor for full drag-and-drop control and Wix Studio for agencies and designers. Wix ADI uses AI to generate a site based on your answers, while Wix AI in 2026 can generate full sites from a single prompt. Wix includes 800+ templates, a 500+ app market, built-in e-commerce, booking, blog, and email marketing tools. Pricing starts at $17 per month for Light and goes up to $159 per month for Business Elite.</p>

    <h2>Templates and Design Quality</h2>
    <p>Templates are the starting point for any site. Squarespace has fewer templates but higher average quality. Wix has more templates but variable quality.</p>

    <h3>Squarespace Templates</h3>
    <p>Squarespace offers around 140 designer-made templates across categories like portfolio, restaurant, store, and blog. Every template is professionally designed, mobile-responsive, and uses a consistent grid system. The unified 7.1 system means you can switch templates without losing content, which lets you iterate on design after launch. For users who want a polished look without design skills, Squarespace templates are the best in the category.</p>

    <h3>Wix Templates</h3>
    <p>Wix offers 800+ templates across more categories, but quality varies. Some templates are professionally designed, while others feel dated or generic. Wix templates are also less interchangeable: once you start editing, switching to a different template means rebuilding the site. The upside is more variety, especially for niche industries like real estate, fitness, and events.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best template quality goes to <span class="winner-tag">Squarespace</span>. Best template variety goes to <span class="winner-tag">Wix</span>.</p>

    <h2>Editor Experience</h2>
    <p>The editor is where you spend most of your time. Squarespace uses a structured grid, while Wix uses free-form drag-and-drop. Both approaches work, but they suit different users.</p>

    <h3>Squarespace Editor</h3>
    <p>Squarespace's editor enforces a grid. You add blocks like text, image, gallery, and form, and the editor aligns them automatically. This produces clean layouts without effort, but it also limits where you can place elements. You cannot overlap text and images or move a button 50 pixels to the left. For users who want a polished result without design skills, the structured grid is a feature.</p>

    <h3>Wix Editor</h3>
    <p>Wix's editor is true drag-and-drop. You can place any element anywhere on the page, overlap elements, and create custom layouts that would be impossible in Squarespace. The trade-off is that you can also create ugly layouts if you do not know what you are doing. Wix also offers a separate mobile editor, so you can adjust the mobile layout independently, which Squarespace does not allow.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best for clean output goes to <span class="winner-tag">Squarespace</span>. Best for custom layout goes to <span class="winner-tag">Wix</span>.</p>

    <h2>Pricing Comparison</h2>
    <p>Pricing depends on whether you need personal, business, or e-commerce features. Wix is cheaper at entry level, while Squarespace is cheaper for e-commerce.</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <tr><th>Tier</th><th>Squarespace</th><th>Wix</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Free</td><td>14-day trial only</td><td>Yes, with Wix branding</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Personal</td><td>$16/mo</td><td>$17/mo (Light)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Business</td><td>$23/mo</td><td>$29/mo (Core)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Basic Commerce</td><td>$28/mo</td><td>$32/mo (Business)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Advanced Commerce</td><td>$52/mo</td><td>$159/mo (Business Elite)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Transaction Fees</td><td>0% on commerce plans</td><td>0% on business plans</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Email Marketing</td><td>Built-in (Email Campaigns)</td><td>Wix Email Marketing add-on</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Custom Domain</td><td>Yes, all paid plans</td><td>Yes, all paid plans</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Annual Billing Discount</td><td>Yes, 25%</td><td>Yes, varies</td></tr>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>For personal sites, Wix Light at $17 per month is comparable to Squarespace Personal at $16 per month, but Wix often runs first-year discounts that bring the effective price below $10. For business and commerce, Squarespace is cheaper: Business at $23 per month versus Wix Core at $29, and Basic Commerce at $28 versus Wix Business at $32.</p>
    <p>For advanced commerce, Wix Business Elite at $159 per month is much more expensive than Squarespace Advanced Commerce at $52, but Wix includes more advanced features like multi-channel selling and custom reports.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best entry-level price goes to <span class="winner-tag">Wix</span>. Best commerce price goes to <span class="winner-tag">Squarespace</span>.</p>

    <h2>E-commerce and Selling</h2>
    <p>If you plan to sell products, e-commerce features matter. Squarespace is better for small stores, while Wix is better for larger stores with custom needs.</p>

    <h3>Squarespace Commerce</h3>
    <p>Squarespace Commerce includes unlimited products, zero transaction fees on commerce plans, integrated inventory, abandoned cart recovery, and customer accounts. It supports digital products, subscriptions, and gift cards. The checkout is clean and on-brand, and inventory syncs with point-of-sale systems like Square. For stores under 100 products with strong visual branding, Squarespace is the better choice.</p>

    <h3>Wix Commerce</h3>
    <p>Wix Commerce includes unlimited products, zero transaction fees on business plans, abandoned cart recovery, and a larger app ecosystem. It supports multi-channel selling across Instagram, Facebook, Amazon, and Google. Wix also offers custom checkout, product reviews, and advanced shipping rules. For stores over 100 products or those needing multi-channel sales, Wix is the better choice.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best for small stores goes to <span class="winner-tag">Squarespace</span>. Best for large stores goes to <span class="winner-tag">Wix</span>.</p>

    <h2>SEO and Performance</h2>
    <p>Both platforms have strong SEO, but Wix offers more granular control.</p>

    <h3>Squarespace SEO</h3>
    <p>Squarespace includes customizable meta titles and descriptions, automatic sitemaps, SSL, clean URLs by default, and structured data for products and blog posts. It also has strong mobile performance because the grid system produces responsive layouts automatically. For most users, Squarespace SEO is sufficient without extra configuration.</p>

    <h3>Wix SEO</h3>
    <p>Wix includes customizable meta tags, automatic sitemaps, SSL, SEO patterns for bulk editing, structured data, and a built-in SEO checklist called Wix SEO Wiz. Wix also offers more granular control over canonical tags, robots.txt, and 301 redirects. For users who want fine-grained SEO control, Wix is the better choice.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best out-of-the-box SEO goes to <span class="winner-tag">Squarespace</span>. Best granular SEO control goes to <span class="winner-tag">Wix</span>.</p>

    <h2>App Ecosystem and Integrations</h2>
    <p>Apps extend what your site can do. Wix has a much larger app market, while Squarespace has a smaller, curated set of integrations.</p>

    <h3>Squarespace Integrations</h3>
    <p>Squarespace includes built-in tools for email marketing, scheduling, memberships, and analytics. It integrates with Stripe, PayPal, Apple Pay, Square POS, and a limited set of third-party apps via Squarespace Extensions. The curated approach means fewer choices but higher quality.</p>

    <h3>Wix App Market</h3>
    <p>Wix has a 500+ app market covering marketing, sales, social, design, and productivity. Popular apps include Wix Bookings, Wix Events, Wix Forum, and integrations with Mailchimp, QuickBooks, and HubSpot. The larger ecosystem means more options but also more variable quality and additional subscription costs.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> <span class="winner-tag">Wix</span> wins app ecosystem.</p>

    <h2>Mobile Experience and Performance</h2>
    <p>Mobile traffic now accounts for over 60% of website visits in 2026, so how a builder handles mobile matters more than ever. Squarespace and Wix take fundamentally different approaches to mobile, and the difference shows up in both editor experience and final site performance.</p>

    <h3>Squarespace Mobile</h3>
    <p>Squarespace uses a single responsive layout per template. When you design on desktop, the template automatically reflows the layout for mobile. There is no separate mobile editor, which means you cannot fine-tune the mobile layout independently. The advantage is consistency: your mobile site always matches your desktop design, and there is no risk of a broken mobile layout caused by a misplaced element. Squarespace templates also score well on Core Web Vitals because the grid system produces lightweight HTML and optimized image loading by default. Lighthouse performance scores typically land between 90 and 100 for Squarespace sites on commerce plans.</p>

    <h3>Wix Mobile</h3>
    <p>Wix offers a separate mobile editor where you can adjust the mobile layout independently from desktop. This is useful when a desktop design does not translate well to a small screen, but it also means you have to maintain two layouts. Wix has improved site performance significantly since 2022, but drag-and-drop sites still tend to produce heavier HTML than grid-based builders. Lighthouse scores for Wix sites typically range from 70 to 90, depending on how many apps and animations are installed. Wix also supports AMP for blog posts, which can improve mobile loading on content-heavy sites.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best out-of-the-box mobile goes to <span class="winner-tag">Squarespace</span>. Best mobile layout control goes to <span class="winner-tag">Wix</span>.</p>

    <h2>Pros and Cons</h2>

    <h3>Squarespace Pros</h3>
    <div class="pros-box">
      <ul>
        <li>Best template quality in the category</li>
        <li>Structured grid editor produces clean layouts automatically</li>
        <li>Unified template system lets you switch designs after launch</li>
        <li>Cheaper commerce plans with zero transaction fees</li>
        <li>Built-in email marketing and scheduling tools</li>
        <li>24/7 chat and email support</li>
        <li>Strong mobile performance with responsive layouts</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h3>Squarespace Cons</h3>
    <div class="cons-box">
      <ul>
        <li>No free tier, only 14-day trial</li>
        <li>Limited app market compared to Wix</li>
        <li>Less granular SEO control</li>
        <li>No drag-and-drop, layout is grid-bound</li>
        <li>Fewer niche industry templates</li>
        <li>Advanced commerce features limited</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h3>Wix Pros</h3>
    <div class="pros-box">
      <ul>
        <li>True drag-and-drop editor for custom layouts</li>
        <li>500+ app market with broad integrations</li>
        <li>Free tier with Wix branding</li>
        <li>More granular SEO control with patterns and structured data</li>
        <li>Separate mobile editor for custom mobile layout</li>
        <li>Multi-channel selling for larger stores</li>
        <li>AI site builder generates full sites from a prompt</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h3>Wix Cons</h3>
    <div class="cons-box">
      <ul>
        <li>Template quality varies, some feel dated</li>
        <li>Cannot switch templates after editing starts</li>
        <li>Free-form editor can produce ugly layouts without skill</li>
        <li>Commerce plans more expensive than Squarespace</li>
        <li>App subscriptions add to monthly cost</li>
        <li>Mobile performance can suffer with heavy custom layouts</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h2>Who Should Use Which?</h2>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Creative Professionals and Portfolios</h3>
      <p>If you are a photographer, designer, or artist building a portfolio, Squarespace is the better choice. Its templates are designer-made, the grid editor produces clean galleries automatically, and the visual quality matches the work you are showcasing. Creative professionals consistently prefer Squarespace for portfolios and brand-led sites.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Small Businesses with Custom Layouts</h3>
      <p>If you are a small business that needs a custom layout that does not fit a template, Wix is the better choice. The drag-and-drop editor lets you place elements anywhere, and the larger app market supports niche needs like booking, events, and membership. Small businesses that need specific layouts and third-party apps consistently prefer Wix.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Restaurants and Hospitality</h3>
      <p>If you run a restaurant, cafe, or hotel, both platforms work, but Squarespace is slightly better for brand-led hospitality sites. Its restaurant templates are among the best in the category, and the integrated scheduling tool handles reservations. Wix is competitive if you need online ordering or a custom menu layout.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Small to Mid-sized Online Stores</h3>
      <p>If you run a store with under 100 products and strong visual branding, Squarespace Commerce is the better choice. It is cheaper, includes zero transaction fees, and the on-brand checkout matches your site. For stores over 100 products or those needing multi-channel selling across Amazon, Instagram, and Google, Wix Commerce is the better choice.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Agencies and Freelancers Building for Clients</h3>
      <p>If you build sites for clients, Wix Studio is the better choice. It is designed for agencies with features like client billing, handover, and reusable templates. Squarespace is suitable for simpler client work but lacks the agency-focused tooling of Wix Studio.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Real-World Use Cases</h2>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Photographer launching a portfolio site</h3>
      <p>A wedding photographer needs a portfolio site with full-bleed image galleries, a contact form, and a blog. Squarespace's portfolio templates are designed for visual work, the grid editor produces clean galleries automatically, and the integrated blog supports SEO-friendly URLs. The photographer launches a polished site in a weekend without hiring a designer. Wix would work but requires more manual layout work to match Squarespace's out-of-the-box polish.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Boutique owner launching a 50-product store</h3>
      <p>A boutique owner launches a store with 50 products across clothing, accessories, and home goods. Squarespace Commerce at $28 per month includes unlimited products, zero transaction fees, integrated inventory, and a clean on-brand checkout. The owner manages inventory from one dashboard and syncs with Square POS for in-store sales. Wix Commerce would work but costs more at $32 per month for similar features.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Yoga studio with class schedules and bookings</h3>
      <p>A yoga studio needs a site with class schedules, online bookings, and a membership area. Wix is the better choice here because Wix Bookings handles recurring classes, instructor profiles, and online payments natively. The drag-and-drop editor lets the studio create a custom layout for each class type. Squarespace has scheduling but less specialized for recurring class-based businesses.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Marketing agency building 20 client sites</h3>
      <p>A marketing agency builds and maintains 20 client sites across industries. Wix Studio is purpose-built for this workflow with client billing, handover tools, reusable templates, and a dashboard to manage all sites. The agency can hand off a site to a client without giving up admin control. Squarespace lacks agency-specific tooling, which makes managing 20 client sites more manual.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Final Verdict</h2>
    <div class="verdict">
      <p><strong>Best overall for portfolios and brand-led sites: Squarespace.</strong> Squarespace at $16 per month for Personal offers the best template quality, cleanest editor, and strongest visual output for portfolios, restaurants, and small brand sites. For users who want a polished site without design skills, Squarespace is the safer and higher-quality choice.</p>
      <p><strong>Best for small businesses with custom needs: Wix.</strong> Wix at $17 per month for Light offers true drag-and-drop, a larger app market, and more granular SEO control. For small businesses that need custom layouts, third-party apps, and multi-channel selling, Wix is the more flexible choice despite slightly higher pricing.</p>
      <p><strong>Best budget choice for e-commerce: Squarespace Commerce.</strong> Squarespace Basic Commerce at $28 per month is cheaper than Wix Business at $32 and includes unlimited products with zero transaction fees. For small to mid-sized stores with strong branding, Squarespace offers the best value.</p>
      <p><strong>Recommended combo:</strong> For users who want both polish and flexibility, start with Squarespace for the main brand site and use Wix for specific landing pages or microsites that need custom layout. Total cost is around $33 per month and covers both polished branding and custom layouts.</p>
    </div>

